{"birds": [{"name": "House Finch", "sci": "Haemorhous mexicanus", "n": 29, "first": "9:03 AM", "last": "9:33 AM", "ts": 1793279029, "mins": [543, 544, 544, 545, 545, 545, 546, 546, 546, 547, 547, 548, 548, 548, 549, 549, 550, 550, 551, 551, 551, 552, 553, 553, 554, 570, 572, 573, 573], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Native to the West, house finches were released on Long Island in 1940 as pet-shop “Hollywood finches” and spread across the East. Redder males are favored by females.", "vox": "sounds/voice/haemorhous-mexicanus.mp3?v=1793113005", "snd": "sounds/haemorhous-mexicanus.mp3?v=1793113108", "sndby": "Ted Floyd, xeno-canto XC622745, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "housefinch", "cm": 14.0, "img": "cutouts/housefinch.png", "w": 800, "h": 581, "len": 662.0, "beak": [0.12, 0.13, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 23, "b": "B+AAAAfwAQoP8AHOH/gB7wf8Af8D/gH/B/8A3wf/AP4H/4zgB//fwAP/zwAD//4AAf/+AAD//AAAf/gAAD/8AA/+/AAH4B4AB+APAB6AD4A4AAeA8AAAAMAAAAA="}}, {"name": "Dark-eyed Junco", "sci": "Junco hyemalis", "n": 1, "first": "9:04 AM", "last": "9:04 AM", "ts": 1793279045, "mins": [544], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Often called “snowbirds” because they show up as winter arrives. The white outer tail feathers flash as they flit away.", "vox": "sounds/voice/junco-hyemalis.mp3?v=1793113009", "snd": "sounds/junco-hyemalis.mp3?v=1793113154", "sndby": "Phoenix Birder, xeno-canto XC563632, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "junco", "cm": 15.5, "img": "cutouts/junco.png", "w": 800, "h": 562, "len": 625.0, "beak": [0.2, 0.22, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 22, "b": "AAAABgAAAAYA8AAMAfgAOAP8ADAD/gBxAf8AZwH/gMwB/8G4Af/j8AD/94AA//4AAH/8AAA//gAAH/4AAAf/AAQPh4AEPwHABfgA4A/QAHA+AAAA8AAAAA=="}}, {"name": "Blue Jay", "sci": "Cyanocitta cristata", "n": 5, "first": "9:05 AM", "last": "9:31 AM", "ts": 1793279124, "mins": [545, 557, 559, 567, 571], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "That blue isn’t a pigment. Tiny structures in the feathers scatter light, which is why a crushed blue feather looks brown. Jays also bury acorns and help spread oak trees.", "vox": "sounds/voice/cyanocitta-cristata.mp3?v=1793112993", "snd": "sounds/cyanocitta-cristata.mp3?v=1793113328", "sndby": "Christopher McPherson, xeno-canto XC691163, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "bluejay", "cm": 28.0, "img": "cutouts/bluejay.png", "w": 385, "h": 369, "len": 474.0, "beak": [0.99, 0.17, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 31, "b": "AAAPgAAAD+AAAA/4AAAH+AAAD/8AAB//AAA//gAAf/AAAP/wAAH/8AAD//AAB//wAB//4AAf/+AAf//gAH//zAD//5wA//94Af+OcAP/8eAD9/fgA/l/gAPgHwAHgD4AHwA4AB8AeAA+AGAAfADgAHgAwADwAAAA4AAAAA=="}}, {"name": "Fish Crow", "sci": "Corvus ossifragus", "n": 20, "first": "9:05 AM", "last": "9:32 AM", "ts": 1793279152, "mins": [545, 546, 546, 547, 552, 552, 554, 556, 556, 557, 565, 566, 566, 566, 567, 567, 567, 568, 571, 572], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "It looks just like an American Crow. The giveaway is the voice: a nasal, two-note “uh-uh” instead of a clear “caw.”", "vox": "sounds/voice/corvus-ossifragus.mp3?v=1793112991", "snd": "sounds/corvus-ossifragus.mp3?v=1793113105", "sndby": "Mike Nelson, xeno-canto XC130581, CC BY-NC-SA 3.0", "key": "fishcrow", "cm": 38.0, "img": "cutouts/fishcrow.png", "w": 800, "h": 474, "len": 557.0, "beak": [0.27, 0.08, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 19, "b": "AH4AAAD+AAAAPwAAAB+AAAAf4AAAH/AAAB/4AAAf/gAAD/4AAAf/AAAD/4AAAf/AAAD34P8Bs+D///Dg/////v////8AD///AAAP/w=="}}, {"name": "Northern Cardinal", "sci": "Cardinalis cardinalis", "n": 1, "first": "9:09 AM", "last": "9:09 AM", "ts": 1793279395, "mins": [549], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Female cardinals sing too, which is unusual for a North American songbird. During courtship the male feeds her seeds beak to beak.", "vox": "sounds/voice/cardinalis-cardinalis.mp3?v=1793112978", "snd": "sounds/cardinalis-cardinalis.mp3?v=1793113085", "sndby": "David Tattersley, xeno-canto XC1000248, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "cardinal", "cm": 22.0, "img": "cutouts/cardinal.png", "w": 516, "h": 348, "len": 530.0, "beak": [0.06, 0.28, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 22, "b": "AYAAAAeAAAAPgAAAH4AAAB/AAAA/4AAAf/gAAB/+AAAf/4AAH//AAA//8AAP//gAB//8AAf//AAD//+AAf//wEB/v/jwf3v+///+/z//vn945gA+MAAAAA=="}}, {"name": "Eastern Bluebird", "sci": "Sialia sialis", "n": 2, "first": "9:14 AM", "last": "9:35 AM", "ts": 1793279674, "mins": [554, 575], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Bluebirds nest in tree cavities, and their numbers crashed in the early 1900s when starlings and house sparrows took the holes. Volunteer nest-box trails helped them rebound.", "vox": "sounds/voice/sialia-sialis.mp3?v=1793113046", "snd": "sounds/sialia-sialis.mp3?v=1793113130", "sndby": "Robin Carter, xeno-canto XC1374, CC BY-NC-ND 2.5", "key": "bluebird", "cm": 18.0, "img": "cutouts/bluebird.png", "w": 800, "h": 609, "len": 744.0, "beak": [0.69, 0.1, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 24, "b": "AAPwAAAH/AAAD/4AAA/4AAAf8AAAH/AAAD/wAAB/+AAAf/gDAP/wDgH/8BwD//B4A//h4Af/54AH/94AD/98AB//4AA/P4AAPH0AAHPhAABvgAAA3gAAAPgAAABwAAAA"}}, {"name": "American Goldfinch", "sci": "Spinus tristis", "n": 2, "first": "9:15 AM", "last": "9:25 AM", "ts": 1793279721, "mins": [555, 565], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "One of the strictest vegetarians among birds. Goldfinches nest late in summer, when thistle seeds are ripe, and swap bright yellow for olive-brown in winter.", "vox": "sounds/voice/spinus-tristis.mp3?v=1793113053", "snd": "sounds/spinus-tristis.mp3?v=1793113090", "sndby": "Will Sweet, xeno-canto XC453934, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "goldfinch", "cm": 12.0, "img": "cutouts/goldfinch.png", "w": 517, "h": 369, "len": 415.0, "beak": [0.18, 0.15, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 23, "b": "APgAAAH4AAAD/AAAB/wAAAP+AAEB/wAHAf+ADgH/wBwB/8DwAf/h4AH/94AA//8AAH/8ABA/+AAAH/wAAB/+AAA/fhAAeA8AA/gHgAfgB8APAANAPAAAADgAAAA="}}, {"name": "White-breasted Nuthatch", "sci": "Sitta carolinensis", "n": 4, "first": "9:15 AM", "last": "9:30 AM", "ts": 1793279743, "mins": [555, 564, 565, 570], "anim": null, "trunk": true, "fact": "Nuthatches climb down tree trunks head first. The name comes from “nut-hack”: they wedge a seed into bark and hack it open.", "vox": "sounds/voice/sitta-carolinensis.mp3?v=1793113050", "snd": "sounds/sitta-carolinensis.mp3?v=1793113095", "sndby": "Andrew Spencer, xeno-canto XC14860, CC BY-NC-ND 2.5", "key": "nuthatch", "cm": 14.0, "img": "cutouts/nuthatch.png", "w": 532, "h": 327, "len": 372.0, "beak": [0.79, 0.42, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 20, "b": "fgAAAH8AAAB/AAAAP4AAAD+AAAAfwAAAH/4gAB///gAf///AB///gAf//gAD//wAA//4AAP/8AAB//AAAf/AAAD+AAAA/gAAAP4AAAB/AAA="}}, {"name": "American Crow", "sci": "Corvus brachyrhynchos", "n": 4, "first": "9:20 AM", "last": "9:23 AM", "ts": 1793280054, "mins": [560, 561, 561, 563], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Crows remember individual human faces for years, and they seem to pass the grudge along to other crows.", "vox": "sounds/voice/corvus-brachyrhynchos.mp3?v=1793112990", "snd": "sounds/corvus-brachyrhynchos.mp3?v=1793113335", "sndby": "David Vander Pluym, xeno-canto XC289011, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "crow", "cm": 45.0, "img": "cutouts/crow.png", "w": 553, "h": 364, "len": 568.0, "beak": [0.94, 0.14, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 21, "b": "AAAHwAAAD/gAAB/+AAB//gAD/+AAD//AAB//wAB//4AB//+AB///AAf//wAP//4AH//4AH//8AD//8AA/x+AAPgPwAbwF/6/f////3///7Bhw5AA"}}, {"name": "Northern Flicker", "sci": "Colaptes auratus", "n": 2, "first": "9:22 AM", "last": "9:22 AM", "ts": 1793280155, "mins": [562, 562], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "A woodpecker that often feeds on the ground, mainly on ants. Eastern flickers flash bright yellow under the wings and tail when they fly.", "vox": "sounds/voice/colaptes-auratus.mp3?v=1793112986", "snd": "sounds/colaptes-auratus.mp3?v=1793113157", "sndby": "Thomas Magarian, xeno-canto XC354617, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "flicker", "cm": 31.0, "img": "cutouts/flicker.png", "w": 800, "h": 579, "len": 732.0, "beak": [0.66, 0.07, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 23, "b": "AA/AAAAP/AAAH/gAAD/AAAB/gAEA/4AHAf+ADgH/wDwD/4DwA/+B4Af/h4AH/x4AD/54AA//4AAf/4AAH98AAD79AAA+8AAAP/AAAH8gAAD+AAAA+AAAAPAAAAA="}}, {"name": "Carolina Wren", "sci": "Thryothorus ludovicianus", "n": 4, "first": "9:25 AM", "last": "9:26 AM", "ts": 1793280351, "mins": [565, 566, 566, 566], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Its song sounds like “teakettle, teakettle, teakettle,” and pairs often sing duets. It will nest in odd places: flowerpots, mailboxes, even old boots.", "vox": "sounds/voice/thryothorus-ludovicianus.mp3?v=1793113060", "snd": "sounds/thryothorus-ludovicianus.mp3?v=1793113075", "sndby": "Jacob Saucier, xeno-canto XC498646, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "wren", "cm": 13.0, "img": "cutouts/wren.png", "w": 800, "h": 633, "len": 781.0, "beak": [0.85, 0.21, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 25, "b": "OAAAADgAAAA8AAAAHgB+AB4B/+APA//wB5//AAf//gAH//4AB//+AAf//AAH//wAD//4AAP/+AAB//H/AP/f/wA///8AGf/cAB/7AA//8AAP/4AAP/8AAP9gAAD8AAAAcAAAAA=="}}, {"name": "White-throated Sparrow", "sci": "Zonotrichia albicollis", "n": 1, "first": "9:26 AM", "last": "9:26 AM", "ts": 1793280363, "mins": [566], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "White-throats come in two color forms, white-striped and tan-striped, and almost always pair with the opposite form. Listen for “Oh-sweet-Canada, Canada, Canada.”", "vox": "sounds/voice/zonotrichia-albicollis.mp3?v=1793113071", "snd": "sounds/zonotrichia-albicollis.mp3?v=1793113140", "sndby": "Matt Wistrand, xeno-canto XC339648, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "whitethroated", "cm": 17.0, "img": "cutouts/whitethroated.png", "w": 800, "h": 460, "len": 599.0, "beak": [0.73, 0.14, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 18, "b": "AAB+AAAB/gAAB/8DAB/+BwB//h4B//54f//84P///8AAP/8AAA/8AAAD8AAAA/AAAA8AAAA/AAAAeAAAAeAAAAeAAAAHAAAA"}}, {"name": "Red-bellied Woodpecker", "sci": "Melanerpes carolinus", "n": 2, "first": "9:28 AM", "last": "9:29 AM", "ts": 1793280520, "mins": [568, 569], "anim": null, "trunk": true, "fact": "The “red belly” is only a faint pink blush you will rarely see. The red cap is the giveaway. Its barbed tongue can reach about two inches past the bill tip.", "vox": "sounds/voice/melanerpes-carolinus.mp3?v=1793113012", "snd": "sounds/melanerpes-carolinus.mp3?v=1793113332", "sndby": "Bruce Lagerquist, xeno-canto XC713255, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "redbellied", "cm": 24.0, "img": "cutouts/redbellied.png", "w": 400, "h": 800, "len": 516.0, "beak": [0.52, 0.23, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 64, "b": ""}}, {"name": "Pileated Woodpecker", "sci": "Dryocopus pileatus", "n": 1, "first": "9:33 AM", "last": "9:33 AM", "ts": 1793280786, "mins": [573], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "It chisels big rectangular holes in dead wood while hunting carpenter ants. Abandoned holes later shelter owls, ducks and other cavity nesters.", "vox": "sounds/voice/dryocopus-pileatus.mp3?v=1793112997", "snd": "sounds/dryocopus-pileatus.mp3?v=1793113171", "sndby": "Mike Nelson, xeno-canto XC102028, CC BY-NC-ND 2.5", "key": "pileated", "cm": 45.0, "img": "cutouts/pileated.png", "w": 649, "h": 800, "len": 737.0, "beak": [0.42, 0.1, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 39, "b": "AAAf/gAAH/4AAB/+Bzwf/x/4H/9/8B///+Af/3/AH/8/8B//D/wf/wf+H/8D/x//Af+f/wP/n/8D/5//A////wH///8B////Af///wH///4A/9/+AP/f/gD/3/4Af5//AH+f/wA/v/4AP7//AB+//wAf//8AD///AAf//wAH//8ABv/+AAD//gAAf/4AAH//AAA//wAAP/8AAD/+"}}], "also": [], "date": "Thursday, October 29", "where": "", "updated": "9:35 AM", "sun": null, "code": "3bb51669b7", "listen": {"state": "on", "since": null, "control": false}}